---
import { LINKS } from '../data/site';
---

<section class="agents" aria-labelledby="agents-title">
  <h2 id="agents-title" class="display">Agents write code.<br />I answer for it.</h2>
  <div class="columns">
    <p>
      Most of my code now starts with a coding agent, and some of these repositories have far more
      lines than I could type alone. I don’t hide that. It’s part of the job, and I build my own MCP
      servers, skills, and review workflows to do it well.
    </p>
    <p>
      The architecture, the invariants, and the decision to ship are mine. So is the failure at 3
      a.m. Agent work goes through the same pull requests, CI, and release gates as mine.
    </p>
    <p>
      This site was built the same way. The simulation at the top is a generic model, not any
      system I run for an employer. Its promises are checked by an auditor that can fail, and the
      suite attacks it with hundreds of random schedules of outages, crashes, lost, doubled, and
      reordered packets on every push. <a href={LINKS.invariants}>Read the tests</a> or <a href={LINKS.source}>the source</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .agents {
    background: var(--ink);
    padding: clamp(4rem, 12vh, 8rem) var(--gutter);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: clamp(2.75rem, 8.4vw, 9rem);
  }

  .columns {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 3rem);
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  a {
    color: var(--flare);
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .columns {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
